00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
you can have data that's in really good condition, it's meeting all the requirements you set for

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
But when you use that data to build an information product or service

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:14.000
It's not necessarily a quality product or service. If it doesn't fit the needs, fill the needs of the users of that information product or service.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.000
So again, as Tom Redman likes to say.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Is the data right? In other words, does the data fit your requirements? But is it the right data for what you're trying to do?

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
So we're going to investigate both of those for the file that just put out there

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
So anyway, we talked about those principles

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.000
And

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Again, in our definition of information quality, the key term

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:53.000
is value. So, what we're trying to do is we're trying to maximize value, minimize risk for the data assets that are in an organization

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.000
And at the same time, we're trying to produce value for the users of that information

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
So it's really all about value.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:12.000
And of course, along with that, as I mentioned, Annette McGilvery likes to use the term trust, but those go hand in hand, right? If you have

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:29.000
an information product or service is producing value for you, then you need to be able to trust that in order for, if it's producing value, it gives you trust. If you trust it, it helps you produce value. So they kind of go hand in hand, but we're going with the value proposition

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.000
So

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
So let's talk a little bit then about the value

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.000
And so, as I just mentioned, information quality

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
It's about relating information to value

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
And again, this is our

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:59.000
definition that we use, maximizing the value of an organization's information assets and making sure that the information products and services are valuable to the people that use them.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
So

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
There's a couple of ways to relate information to value

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:24.000
I like to use pictures for this. So in any organization, you're going to have all info. I think it's virtually true that every organization that I can think of uses data. So there's some use of data in any organization

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.000
And any organization is going to have some kind of problems

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:41.000
And generally there's an intersection there, it may be true that some problems are not related directly to data. Maybe they're cultural problems. They're having problems with communication

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:56.000
But in general, what we're looking for is the intersection between business problems and the data that's running the business. I say business, but it could be an organization, right? We talked about the three value propositions

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:11.000
There's for-profit, that would be a business. But there's also quality of life, like in a hospital or research center. And then there's also mission accomplishments. So I want to talk generally about organizations and their value. So the value might be

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Not necessarily money, but it could be in accomplishing their given mission

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:24.000
But anyway, so what we want to investigate is say you go into an organization, you're graduated and you

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.000
data quality analyst, and you've been hired by this company

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
And so

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.000
they're having problems, so… so the first step is this

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:44.000
We need to understand what's the intersection between the data and those problems. And so this gives us two ways to go about it.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.000
So one way is that we can start with a business issue

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.000
Okay, every organization has some kind of an issue that it's facing. Then the first step would be

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:04.000
Is it related to the data in the organization? And it may not be, but oftentimes that it is

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:19.000
So one way to start is you go to the managers, you go to the executives, and they know, okay, we're having this problem, we're not getting our invoices out to the customers properly

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:27.000
We're not getting payments. We have a problem retaining customers. We have some kind of a business issue

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Then the next step would be to look and is there a cause, is there a root cause of this problem in the data? Is something in the data causing this?

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Once I know that, then I can improve the data quality, right? So again, we're making the distinction between information quality, data quality

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:54.000
There's something wrong with the condition of this not meeting the requirements that are needed to produce

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:04.000
to solve the problem that we're having, if it's the invoicing, maybe the addresses are wrong, maybe they're out of date, maybe they're incomplete

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:12.000
So once we know it's related to data and we investigate the quality of the data in terms of meeting requirements

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
Does it have all the information that we need

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:20.000
Then we can improve once we improve that quality, we can improve the business results.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:27.000
So this is one way of looking at the issue is starting with the business issue

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Seeing if it's related to data, solving the data problem, and then, hopefully addressing the business issue.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.000
That's one way to do it. The other way to do it

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:46.000
is we start with the data quality assessment. In other words, we go in, you're new

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:56.000
nobody's given you any predictive problem yet, but you look at all the data systems that are running, and you say, okay, well, what's the condition of the data? Do we have

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:01.000
Data quality requirements for the data, are the data really meeting those requirements

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:09.000
So you could start with the data quality assessment and say, are the data really meeting the requirements that we've set for the data

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.000
And if we discover that there are data quality issues, that is

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.000
The data are not in alignment with our requirements

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:22.000
Then we can address those

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:34.000
And then, well, first, then we say, this is very important, by the way, I didn't mean to skip this step. Once we find that there's some data quality anomalies

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.000
That does not automatically mean that there's a business problem.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:44.000
I can guarantee you I can go into any organization that has a relational database system

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:49.000
And find many tables that are completely null. There's nothing in them.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:04.000
But it's not necessarily impacting the business because they're not using that data. They're not using those columns, okay? So, the very important step is once we discover that data have some kind of anomaly, it's not meeting requirements.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.000
The next step is determine then is that having an impact on the business

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
It's the opposite of what we said before

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:21.000
Is bad data causing a problem with the business? And if it is, of course, then we can improve the data quality and improve our business results.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.000
So this happens oftentimes in both ways

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:42.000
In the old days, they used to say, when it was very data quality oriented, they would say, okay, we have all these data quality requirements, we go in and we assess all of our data, but that can be very time consuming and labor intensive to go in

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:55.000
and assess all the data when not necessarily… you'll find… I mean, you may find problems, but even those data quality problems may not have any impact on the business or on the organization.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.000
So

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:15.000
not advising you to go either in any particular way. It could work either way, but oftentimes it's most fruitful to start with a business impact, something that's going wrong with the organization that you can relate to data, then that gives you a specific target of things to fix

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.000
If you just go sweeping through the data looking for data quality problems

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:27.000
That's good, but you want to hold back and not say, you know, well, we want all perfect data in our systems

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:39.000
Because again, making it perfect is not necessarily going to improve your business results if the data are not involved in the business or in some significant part of the business.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.000
So it's good to look at this in both ways.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.000
So

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.000
These are the two views of how to relate information to value.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:04.000
Starting with the business problem that's related to data, that you can fix the data to improve the business, or you can look for defects in the data

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:11.000
determine if those defects are impacting the business. If they are, you can fix them and improve business results.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.000
So there's lots of places to find information value.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:21.000
Obviously, we think often of financial like the examples of the invoices going out

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:34.000
But… and that can affect, you know, operating costs, revenue, cash flow, charges, budget, compliance. This is a big area. Many organizations

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:41.000
have external agencies that require them to comply to certain

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:54.000
requirements. For example, we have that here at the university. We have to report everything into the Arkansas Department of Education. We're getting ready to make a big report now that

00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:10.000
We're see the final enrollments in our courses, we have to report all that. So we have a lot of regulation related to education. Certainly, that's true in medical, but especially in financial organizations, you have a lot of issues

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:14.000
That have to be reported

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.000
Government, industry, privacy

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.000
confidence, trust, so this this gets back to the

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.000
issue, or not the issue, but the

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:34.000
Danette McGilvery that raises, that you're really trying to build trustworthy data in your organization

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:44.000
People trusted. If they don't trust it, they won't use it, and if it's not used, it won't create any value. So, as I mentioned last time

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:53.000
You know, we say that you have to be careful how you say it. I said it wrong to Doug Laney one time, is that

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Data only produces value

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:01.000
when it's used, okay

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:08.000
And so the key word there is produce. Data has value even when you're not using it, has potential value

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.000
But the value is only realized when you actually use it for something

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.000
like, making decisions or controlling systems

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:26.000
Forecasting that can be a very important thing that you do with data as well as just general reporting. And then satisfaction.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:32.000
If you're using… you're in a business, especially where you have customers

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Then, you know, customers are at the top of the pyramid. They need to be, if you want to be successful, you need to have good customer satisfaction. They call customer experience

00:14:46.000 --> 00:15:01.000
And data has a lot to do with that, right? So I don't know if you've had this experience of going into a website and trying to order something or buy something, and you go through a bunch of pages and things and they ask you things that you don't know exactly how to answer

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.000
Maybe you have that experience with Workday trying to register

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:09.000
I know I have a lot of bad experiences with work

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:17.000
So that's poor customer experience or poor customer satisfaction. And oftentimes that's related to data

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:22.000
Suppliers, employees, so there are many ways that you can find

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:31.000
If you use information to create value. As we mentioned, your risk, you want to minimize risk

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:37.000
You want to meet all the regulatory requirements, credit, investment

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Competitiveness, this is another thing that's sort of an intangible, but it's an extremely important value is to be

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:54.000
Part of customer satisfaction, how are you competing with other organizations that are in your same field?

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.000
And again, data can have an important part in that

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:04.000
Leakage, of course, this is what a lot of people think about immediately when we think about risk

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:12.000
We have sensitive data that gets out of our control, right?

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
Hackers get in steal

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:18.000
It was often said that there's two kinds of companies

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.000
Those companies

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:27.000
that have had their personalized or customer information exposed or hacked

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:30.000
And those that don't know it yet

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:39.000
That everybody, to some extent, has been hacked or leaked information that they wanted to keep private

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Productivity, we often think about now in the age of AI, this is the key word, right?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.000
We want to use these large language models

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:01.000
To improve our workflows through agents, right? So Agentic AI is the big thing right now. And agents are like little employees inside of the LLM

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:08.000
that can do work, and so we can be potentially more productive through data

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.000
Namely through these

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Large language models, which require some kind of context, which is also data

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.000
And then

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:23.000
Health, we talked about the other value propositions

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.000
In healthcare disease control treatments

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:29.000
mental treatment, diet, fitness

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:37.000
Safety, this is another big area, right? We're seeing a lot of that now in environmental protection

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:49.000
safety issues, accident prevention, a lot of that goes along with now we have self-driving cars. We're trying to make things safer. We're trying to study traffic flows

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:52.000
We're trying to assess the safety of

00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:00.000
of automobiles. So everywhere you look there's data involved and you if you're making the right

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.000
Making that data into the right information products and services, you can create value. So there are many, many places to find it.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.000
Now

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000
So

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.000
Just as an aside

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:21.000
If value is a very simple formula, right? So value is the difference

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:25.000
between benefits and cost

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:34.000
So if I'm going to undertake some enterprise, which is doing something, I'm going to do something, there's some cost involved

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.000
It could be money, it could be time

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:43.000
It could be wear and tear, it could be a lot of things. There's something you have to give

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:55.000
In terms of cost to get some… to do your action, whatever it is you're trying to do. And the idea is that when you get finished doing it, like building a product, or

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:00.000
You know you're gonna fix up your house, you're gonna do something

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:12.000
it's going to cost you something to do that, but the idea is you're going to realize some benefit out of it. It'll give you some benefit. And of course, what we would like is for that benefit

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:22.000
the value of the… or the benefit to be greater than the cost that it required to get that benefit, and that's what the value is. The value's the difference

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:28.000
And so we have a very simple formula for measuring that. We call it return on investment

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:41.000
And it applies to all kinds of data issues, financial issues, medical issues, whatever endeavor that you want to think about. We can think of it in terms of return on investment

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:46.000
And it's the net gain calculated as a percentage of the amount invested.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:58.000
In other words, what was my gain that I got? How much were the benefits above the cost and then divide that by the cost, and that gives me my return on investment.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:04.000
So the return on investment expressed as a percentage, we multiply by 100 to get percentage

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:11.000
Is the net gain divided by the cost that it took to get that net gain

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:19.000
So we always want that to be positive, right? So why would we want to do something, invest time, money, all types of costs

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:32.000
and get a benefit that was smaller than the cost that it required to get that benefit. We always want the benefit to be larger than the cost to get the benefit, and that would be a positive ROI.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.000
So

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:42.000
And it's a good way to compare alternative opportunities. So in business, we use the return on investment all the time, because typically we have

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:54.000
many opportunities to improve things, right? And we can't do everything at once, so we have to make decisions about, do we want to do improvement A or improvement B, or improvement C

00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:01.000
And so one way to help evaluate is calculate the return on investments of each one of those opportunities.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.000
So

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:08.000
Also, if we're looking at anything long-term

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
Longer than a year

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:15.000
then we really should use discounted values for cost and revenue

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:18.000
And so what do we mean by that

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:23.000
So if I can get a 5% annual return on my investment

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:28.000
What is today's present value of $100 payment

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.000
To me one year from now, so

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:39.000
The idea of an annual return on investment is that money should be working all the time

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:47.000
Money shouldn't be sitting idle in a savings account or not. Under your mattress at home.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:22:03.000
Just sitting there because money needs to be working. You need to be investing it. So if we have some general idea of what the working value of money should be, in this case we're calling it annual return, say it's 5%

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:09.000
So the way to think about this, suppose that somebody says, okay, I'm going to pay you $100.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.000
But I'm not going to pay it until one year from now

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:29.000
So the question becomes, what's the value of $100 one year from now? So we have to back up and say, well, what would I need to, how much money would I need today so that it would grow to $100 at a 5% interest rate

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.000
So that's a pretty easy one to solve

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:43.000
no matter how much money do I need today to have $100 at the end of one year? And of course, they're very easy to solve. We say, well, I've got

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:52.000
the X that I'm looking for is the amount of money I need today, but… but that money plus 5% gain in that money

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:57.000
I want, in one year to equal $100

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.000
So it's a simple algebraic formula

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.000
I'm basically going to take $100 and divide it by 1.05.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:11.000
And so that tells me that basically $100 a year from now

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:20.000
is only worth $95 today if my annual growth of money is expected to be at least 5%.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:23.000
Okay, so that's called the discounted value.

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:31.000
Another place where that comes in, well, we'll talk about that in a minute. So we call that the net present value of money

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:46.000
In other words, again, the basic assumption is that money should constantly be growing in value, and we call that the annual discount rate, like 5% in the previous example. And it will vary from industry to industry or type of business

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:50.000
But you should have some idea of the annual discount rate

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:55.000
Because uninvested money loses value. Put the money in your

00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:09.000
safety deposit box or under your mattress at home, then it's basically losing because if you could, on the average get 5% return, or even 3% return in a savings account

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:15.000
That's where it should be, working and getting and gaining money over time, or gaining value over time.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:21.000
So, future dollars are worth less today, is the basic idea.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:27.000
So here's where this comes up a lot. I don't know how many of you play the lotto or play the

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:34.000
These jackpot prizes. But if you read the fine print, here's how it goes. Hey

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:44.000
play the lotto, and if you win, you'll get a million dollars, okay? One million dollar jackpot prize. But then you read the fine print, and it says, but

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:52.000
What happens if you win the lotto, yes, we're going to give you $100,000 today

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:57.000
And then $100,000 a year for nine years

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:08.000
100,000 today, then another payment next year of 100,000, then the year after that 100,000 and so forth. And so it's a million dollars, that's what they say. Well, it is

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:14.000
If you just look at it, but if you put time into it, it's not worth a million dollars

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:19.000
Let's just suppose for argument that the discount rate is 10%.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:27.000
Pretty high, but say that money could in general gain 10% a year in value

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:33.000
Then that prize money, a million dollars, paid out like that over 9 years

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:37.000
is only worth $675,000 today

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:48.000
And that, by the way, there are many companies that are engaged in this, right? If you win one of these lotteries where they have that, where you're required to take the money out in payments

00:25:48.000 --> 00:26:02.000
Then there are many companies around that will come in and say, hey, I saw that you won this. We'll give you the cash today. And this is how they figure out how much cash to give you. So their business would be, say you won this.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:15.000
And say, okay, well, don't worry, we'll give you $500,000 today, but you give us the annuity. In other words, you give us those nine years of payments and we'll take those payments and we'll

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:24.000
But we'll give you the discounted value minus some profit today

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:34.000
So, by the way, there's thank God for Xcel, right? So XL has a nice formula for net present value that's called NPV

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:44.000
And so what we can do is we can do it this way. We can say, okay, the first $100,000 is $100,000 because I got it today

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:50.000
But then I can say, I go into my Excel formula of NPV and say, okay

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:55.000
My annual growth rate is 10%, so that's the 0.1.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:02.000
And then I just followed that with a series of payments for each year, so there's $100,000 for the

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
At the end of the second year

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:09.000
The third year, fourth year, so there's nine of those. I just plugged those into that formula

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:15.000
And it comes out, that's how I get the $675,000. This is the current value

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:29.000
Then that one is the one where if I have uneven payments that I need to list what payment it is. But if all the payments are the same like this, all the payments are the same amount, $100,000

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:36.000
I can use a different Excel formula called PV, present value, and I can just say this. I can say

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:43.000
Okay, the discount rate is 10% again. There's 10 payments of $100,000

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:51.000
And then the zero says that I don't count the first one. The first one is an immediate payment, just like in my previous formula

00:27:51.000 --> 00:28:03.000
And so when I do that, I get the same result, but I don't have to list out each individual payment, because all the payments are the same

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:08.000
Everybody on board with the present value of money?

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:12.000
All right, so here's an example

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:16.000
So there's a not-for-profit company

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:29.000
And they have a problem with inconsistent reference tables across their operating unit. And it's creating real cost in correcting erroneous shipments all the time because their reference tables are bad

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:35.000
So, they have a solution. They want to set up a single point of truth, what we call master data

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:38.000
or SPOT, single point of truth

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:43.000
corporate reference, so that every… everybody uses the same reference data

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:51.000
And they do some analysis and the cost, it's going to take four years to implement this fully. It's a very large company

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:56.000
And it'll… the cost will be $120,000 a year

00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:04.000
The benefits over four years are going to be $160,000

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:19.000
So if I just look at that without bringing time into consideration, my benefit is I'm going to benefit $160,000. It's going to cost me $120. So if I just use my simple return on investment

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:31.000
I take the 160 minus the 120, that's my net benefit and divide that by the cost that it takes to get it. And so I get a 33% return on my investment

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:37.000
That's just looking at it without time value over four years. That's just

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:45.000
money now, in the present. But let's look at… it's become a little more complicated here

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:54.000
Because the money that I spend, the cost and the benefits that I get don't necessarily come in an even way

00:29:54.000 --> 00:30:01.000
So for example, in one scenario, I have to spend right off the bat

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:08.000
$45,000 in the first year, but it's only going to benefit me $10,000 in the first year.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:14.000
But in the second year, I can get to spend less. 35,000

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:18.000
And then the second year, I'll get a $20,000 benefit

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:31.000
And then, in the third year, I'd only have to spend $25,000, and I got a $40,000 benefit, and in the fourth year, I'd only spend $15,000, and I get a $90,000 benefit.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:41.000
That scenario is the same overall in that my total expenditures is 120 and my total benefit is 16

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:51.000
But the benefit that I get each year and the cost that I have each year are not constant.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:31:04.000
And then I look at the second scenario, then it varies again and I say, okay, well, my first year I have to spend $45,000, but right away I get a $90,000 benefit

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:09.000
And then the next year, I spent 35,000 and I get a $40,000 benefit

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:14.000
And this one I'm sort of getting diminishing returns

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:27.000
And then the third year I spent 25 and I only get 20 back. In the third year, a fourth year, I spend 15 and get 10 back. Again, overall, it's the same cost and benefit

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.000
That all adds up to $120,000 in cost

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.000
And $160,000 in benefit

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:48.000
And then finally, the third scenario is sort of the opposite. My costs are very low at the beginning, and my benefits are high, but it goes the other way. So year one, it's $15,000 cost $90,000 benefit

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:57.000
25 in the next year of my cost, and then 40, so forth and so on. Again, all three of these scenarios are $120,000 in cost

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:02.000
And $160,000 in benefit, but

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:10.000
The return on investment, because if I take present value into account

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:14.000
Then I can see this, that in scenario one

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:20.000
Again, all the returns without using time are 33

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:35.000
But if I take the net present value, they vary dramatically. So in scenario one, my net present value for that project is only 26% return on investment

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:44.000
Whereas in scenario two, it's a 36% return on investment. In Scenario 3, it's 40%.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:33:00.000
And we can see the reason why, right? Look in scenario one, most of my costs are closer in time. My high costs are now, and my benefits are farther out, and those benefits farther out are worthless to me now when I'm planning this project.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:06.000
So scenario one has the lowest return on investment using net present value

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:12.000
Because I have upfront costs and outs, and my benefits are farther out.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:17.000
Which is exactly the opposite of scenario 3, where I have my cost

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:32.000
come later, and so those costs are worth less now. They have less value now because they're farther out in time, but I'm getting my benefits now, early on, in the early years. So that means now my return on investment is actually much larger

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:34.000
It's 40

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:42.000
Whereas where they're pretty much even in scenario two, my costs and my benefits are pretty much go linearly and hand in hand

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:48.000
It's very close to the actual, if I did without time

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:55.000
Without using time values, 33% in scenario two, it's just 36%. So it's a little better

00:33:55.000 --> 00:34:07.000
So this gives you then the reason that in looking at any kind of a long-term project, you really need to look at the present value of money to get a good assessment of what's going on.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:15.000
And this is all done assuming an annual discount rate of 5%.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:20.000
Any questions or comments on them

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:24.000
You'll have an exercise like that that you can be able to do

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:29.000
So making the business case for information quality

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:36.000
So it's really important to have that business case, right? Because if we think that information quality

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:48.000
I hope you think that because it's part of our curriculum is value, then we need to make a business case for. We want to think of information quality

00:34:48.000 --> 00:35:04.000
the way people think about it right now is it's just about cleaning up dirty data. It's just like the janitor has to come in and clean up the floor. There's no benefit, there's no value to it, other than it's just something that needs to be done.

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:19.000
But we want to change that thinking to people to think about information quality and improving quality as an investment. Yeah, we're going to spend some money to do it, but the benefits that we get out of it are going to be larger than that cost

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:22.000
So that's what a business case does.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:30.000
It needs to be a way to say that information quality improvement

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.000
will be successful and create value

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.000
And so it will help you

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:45.000
Get people to thinking about information quality improvement as an investment, and not just an added expense. Because even though there's expense

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:49.000
It will return back more value than it cost

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:52.000
And that helps you gain support from management

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:59.000
And that helps you get the resources you need to have a successful project. So they all work together

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:09.000
So in order to do that you really need to understand what are the business goals or the organizational goals. I keep using business. I probably should change this

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:19.000
Because again, it's true for business, but it's also true for health care. It's true for government organizations, nonprofit organizations

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:23.000
They have goals, they have things they're trying to accomplish

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.000
And you need to align with that

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:29.000
And so, you need to make a clear connection between

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:37.000
The information quality improvement and achieving those goals and objectives of the organization

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:44.000
And in order to do that, and this is something I hope that you will take away from this course

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:52.000
is what we call the Information Quality Context. So again, it's not just about data. I mean, data is super important

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:56.000
And we want to process data into some information product or service

00:36:56.000 --> 00:37:03.000
But in addition to the data, there are 3 other things that we have to be aware of and take into consideration

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:10.000
In general, but also in particular in our business case. We have to understand the data, of course

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:18.000
What's in it, what data items are the most important? We need to really understand the data we're using. But there are three other very important things

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:28.000
Well, in understanding the data, one of the things we often look at is what are the critical data elements? Some elements of the data are more important than others

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:32.000
And we call those CDEs or critical data elements

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:42.000
And the way we can tell if something is critical is what would happen to our process or our business if that particular element was gone

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:48.000
How would it impact our processes? How much would it cost to go recover that and so forth

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:54.000
But now, the other three things besides the data are the people

00:37:54.000 --> 00:38:06.000
The people that are using the data, the people that are manipulating the data, that are transforming the data into some product or service, we can't leave the people up.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:15.000
Oftentimes, many of the issues that we have are people issues. The reason we have this course here called Project and Change Management

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:25.000
Where change managers is about how do you change people's thinking about data and information, and how it's being used.

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:34.000
So in addition to the data, we also need to be very aware of the people that are processing the data, that are using the data, that are managing the data

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:40.000
Also, what are the processes that are being used to transform the data into some product or service?

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:43.000
We need to understand those processes

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:48.000
And then finally, we need to understand the technology. What particular software is being used

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:55.000
Now, this is different than the process, right? We have a process of how we want to change the data

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:58.000
But the tools that we use to change it

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:14.000
can vary, right? So we might be using IBM, we might be using Informatica, SAP. And the way they execute processes are different and some might be more efficient than others for particular types of processes that we're applying to the data.

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:29.000
So we always need to keep in mind in any data scenario, anything related to data, there's obviously the data we need to consider, but we also need to consider the processes, the technology, and the people that are involved.

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:38.000
Those are the four principal components of the context of any data issue.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:51.000
And as we said before, there may be lots of opportunities to make improvements in different ways. So again, this is where the ROI and the other thing, the business cases come into consideration

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:54.000
Which one should we

00:39:54.000 --> 00:40:01.000
go after. We know that for each opportunity, it has some value, which is the benefits less the cost

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:09.000
And again, benefits could mean a lot of things, increasing revenue, lowering operational costs, avoiding

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:14.000
risk of fines and penalties and cost might be mean

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:19.000
additional software or additional personnel. So it could be lots of things

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:26.000
But we try to gather those together and estimate those to weigh the alternatives

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:31.000
And we often use what's called a cost-benefit matrix

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:38.000
So we're going to build a two-dimensional matrix where the vertical, the y-axis, represents the benefit

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:43.000
And the x-axis, or the horizontal axis, represents the cost

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:48.000
And where we want to place these opportunities are in these quadrants

00:40:48.000 --> 00:41:00.000
So the upper left quadrant, we place those opportunities that have a high benefit and a low cost. That means that would be our first choice, right? We can get a good benefit, but it doesn't cost us much to get it

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.000
So those would be top-ranked ones

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:06.000
The second rank

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:11.000
We have a choice here. We could go down and say, well

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:21.000
We want low cost, but we're not going to get so much benefit. But we always want to try to get the most benefit that we can. So really, our second choice is this quadrant

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:26.000
This is where we get high benefit, but it costs us more to get that benefit

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:32.000
But it's important, it's second choice, because it's a high benefit overall

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:42.000
Then our third choice would be here. This is where the benefit is lower, but the cost is low. So this is an easy one in both in terms of cost and benefit

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:54.000
And that would be our third choice. And then our last choice would be those that the overall benefit is not that great, and it's going to cost us quite a bit to get there, but we will get some positive benefit out of it

00:41:54.000 --> 00:42:01.000
So that would be our fourth choice. So this gives us a way to map out different opportunities that we have

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:06.000
To create value with information

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:09.000
But we want to write up a business case

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:20.000
And qualitative measures are useful, but it also… but it's really important to try to include some quantitative financial justification. If it's about value

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:29.000
Then we either need to show that we're going to make more money out of it, or we're going to have a lot more benefit for

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:33.000
Let's say it's medical

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:35.000
Sorry

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:48.000
That it's gonna be very important in treating some disease or some condition, or if it's a mission incompetence really going to help us complete our mission in a much better way than we were doing it before.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:54.000
So, we need some quantitative how to express those quantitatively, if we can at all

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:58.000
And so then that

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:06.000
needs to clearly explain how the benefits of the project is going to exceed the cost that it requires to get there.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:21.000
That is, the project will create value, so we want to sell that project. We want to say this is an investment you need to make. It's not just a cost that we need to undertake, it's an investment in our organization.

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:26.000
So here's an example

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:33.000
Qualitative type case would be to say, well, okay, we need to do project A because it's going to improve

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:41.000
Our information product, some X product that and that will increase customer satisfaction

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:48.000
That's very qualitative because there's no numbers there, there's no percentage, nothing. It just says

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:54.000
This is a good thing to do. We need to improve that because it's going to make our customers happier.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:56.000
Yeah, so we should do it

00:43:56.000 --> 00:44:01.000
Well, okay. So you're wanting me to spend money for this, okay

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:17.000
Whereas we look at it more quantitatively, okay, so yeah, this project's going to improve customer satisfaction, but in a way that we expect that it will increase customer retention by 10% a year

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:25.000
In other words, we'll lose, we won't lose as many customers each year as we do now. We're going to improve that loss rate

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.000
We're gonna reduce it. So by 10%

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:39.000
And now the call… and so this is something that accountants in a business are always going to know. They're going to say, well, we know the cost to acquire a new customer

00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:46.000
We have to do advertising, we have to do onboarding, we have to do a lot of things to get the customer in to our customer base

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:56.000
And we estimate that to cost $1,200 a customer for a new customer. However, to service and maintain an existing customer

00:44:56.000 --> 00:45:04.000
to do their accounts, to do their billing, all that only costs us about $400 a year.

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:10.000
So now, if I have 2000 customers currently in my business

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:15.000
then I can make a calculation of how… what would be the impact

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:17.000
of having

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:22.000
increasing our customer retention by 10% a year

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:35.000
So for 2,000 customers, I go through the formula. I've got 2,000 customers, I've got 10% retention. I've got the difference between acquiring them and losing them

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:40.000
is $800, right? 1200 minus the 400

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.000
That's $160,000 a year

00:45:43.000 --> 00:46:00.000
So I can use that to say, okay, if my cost of doing this project is less than 160,000, then this is a winning formula, right? Because I could say, well, we're going to spend $50,000 to do this

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:06.000
But we're going to get $160,000 back

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:13.000
So, again, the point of this is it's important, even though we're making estimates, or guesstimates sometimes

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:27.000
It's important to have the numbers there and in business they call this a pro forma, right? In other words, I'm going to start a new business or I'm proposing a project. I prepare this and say, okay, over the years, over time.

00:46:27.000 --> 00:46:36.000
How much am I going to be spending? What do I expect to get back? Show me the money, show me the money that's going to come back to me by doing this.

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:42.000
So we consider all the factors. In addition to analyzing benefit and cost

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:45.000
We need to think about how much time that will take

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:52.000
Will it have a long-term benefit? Is it just something going to be a short-term fix, or a long-term

00:46:52.000 --> 00:47:00.000
What's the risk of failing? Okay, we say we're going to do this project, but could it possibly fail and we not get any return on it?

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:13.000
And do we have the right resources to do it? Are there enough people in the right roles to carry out the project? Do we have the right IT structure, the right data experts, project leaders, so forth?

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:21.000
So and then also how visible would the project be? How will people really see the results and understand this

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:26.000
And that's something we don't do a lot in information quality, sort of blow our own horn

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:31.000
to say, you know, look what we were able to do for the company

00:47:31.000 --> 00:47:35.000
So that brings us down to

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:41.000
Some of the characteristics of information that's going to get us into our next project

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:43.000
Our next assignment

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:53.000
So the information lifecycle now. So information, just like everything, has a life cycle to it.

00:47:53.000 --> 00:48:03.000
It starts out somewhere, we acquire the data, we get it, we use it, and at some point, it's not useful to us anymore.

00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:08.000
In some data lasts longer than other data

00:48:08.000 --> 00:48:10.000
For example.

00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:13.000
We think about timeliness of

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:16.000
Stock quotes right

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:27.000
Stock quotes don't last for only a few seconds, right? Because they're changing constantly. So what good does it do for you to give me yesterday's stock quotes? That's not what the stock's worth today, right?

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:31.000
So the life cycle of stock quotes is very, very short

00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:34.000
Other information like

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:42.000
You know, customer information. I used to work at Axiom Corporation. We used to kid each other. We would buy a lot of information.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:47.000
And we'd say, well, you know, how many times do we need to buy somebody's date of birth

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.000
Well, it doesn't typically change

00:48:49.000 --> 00:49:04.000
So that has a very long life cycle. As long as that person is potentially a customer or a patient, then their date of birth is a valuable piece of information that we can use to understand their age and other things about

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:07.000
About our customer

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:14.000
So, so life cycle, all data has life cycle, but it varies in length, but it all has

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:17.000
Certain characteristics or stages that it goes through

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:25.000
The first part is to plan for the information before we ever get the information, is this the information that we even need?

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:28.000
Why are we getting this information?

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:45.000
And how much is it going to cost us to acquire the information? We think about how, okay, this is information that we want, or data that we want, how do we get it? Is it data that we're going to have to compile ourselves? Are we going to get it from other sources and have to transform it some way

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:53.000
Are we going to lease it? Are we just going to borrow the data and lease it? A lot of the information we used at Axiom

00:49:53.000 --> 00:50:00.000
was licensed information, really, we didn't really buy it. We just leased it and used it

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:05.000
And they kept giving us fresher and fresher information over time. So it's like a service

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:14.000
And then once we get the information, we've got to do something with it. We've got to store it.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:16.000
Excuse me. And

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:21.000
Sure, right? People need… the people that need it need to have access to it

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:27.000
So we have to figure out how we're going to store it, how we're going to make it available to the people that are going to be using it

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:30.000
And then we have to maintain it. Generally

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.000
Stock quotes aside, if we're talking about like customer information

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:45.000
People move, right? So if we're going to contact them at their address, whether that's a physical address or an email address

00:50:45.000 --> 00:51:01.000
or some social tag or some phone number, those tend to change, right? And so to maintain that information, we need to keep up with that and be sure that we have the most current information that we need for whatever product or service that we're building.

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:06.000
So that's important. Then, of course, at some point, we want to use it

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:13.000
We want to build some information product or service that's going to create that value that we're looking for

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:25.000
And then at some point, we're going to dispose. We won't need it anymore, right? We might want to archive it, or we might act… in some cases, we may be required to actually get rid of it, delete it

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:38.000
certain financial information. The government says you must retain it for seven years, but at the end of seven years, you must destroy it. So, all of those are regulatory things we have to be aware of

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:44.000
And you'll notice that there's 6 things here

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:49.000
All six of those cost… there's cost involved with all of those

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:53.000
However, only one of them

00:51:53.000 --> 00:52:04.000
Brings us back any benefit, any value. And that's the apply, right? Only when we get to the point that we're actually using that data for some purpose

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:13.000
And getting some value out of it, we're getting some return on it. So that's a heavy burden to bear. The apply phase has to carry all other phases

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:24.000
All other faces cost without giving us any benefit, but the apply is the only place we get benefits. So that's the reason, again, for a business case, we need to be very sure

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:29.000
If we're going to undertake a project involving data that

00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:38.000
the return on investment is going to be positive, right? That we're going to get back more than it cost us to do all these things in the life cycle

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:43.000
I like this one because I have bad memory and I can remember that it's called POSMAD.

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:53.000
You take the first letters of each one of those, plan, obtain, store, maintain, apply, and disposeman. And I borrowed that

00:52:53.000 --> 00:53:03.000
Danette McGilvery, who's very big in the data quality field, she has a book out called Executing Data Quality Projects.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:08.000
Very well regarded in the industry.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:23.000
So again, it's not a linear process, even though I listed them in order. If we look at it sort of in a two-dimensional way, certainly we start with planning and obtaining it till we get the data. But then once we get there, we store it and share it

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:35.000
then maintaining and applying could be in a cycle, right? So we need to be constantly maintaining the data, gross data requires some maintenance. And then we're applying it periodically, right?

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:43.000
We're building every month, or we're standing up a website, and people are using it in real time, 24 hours a day

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:51.000
in applying where they're using that data that we're providing, and we're constantly maintaining. So it's not linear in that sense.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:55.000
Although at some point, then we need to dispose of it.

00:53:55.000 --> 00:53:58.000
The life cycle is not linear

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:06.000
The cost vary, and the application of the data vary too. And actually, we could be getting

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:16.000
For example, at Axon, we're getting data all the time. We planned for data, but then once we got a license, they would give us a feed every… typically every month, sometimes every week

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:26.000
That would have to be stored, we'd have to maintain it, apply it constantly. So this is a very cyclical, and not just a linear process.

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:34.000
And so again, I won't go into a lot of detail on all these because we're close

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:36.000
But

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:41.000
Again, it's very important to prepare for getting data

00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:50.000
For example, again, use an Axiom example, a lot of the data that we would get, which was licensed data or leased data

00:54:50.000 --> 00:55:01.000
had restrictions on it. We could only use it for certain things, so it didn't do us any good to pay $20,000 to get a data feed to create some kind of an index

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:13.000
that wasn't allowed for in the data. The data could, for example, only be used for marketing. It couldn't be used to build some kind of reference data that we would use over and over.

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:19.000
So, you have to be aware of what any restrictions that might be on the data in your planning

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:24.000
And, you know, what's the condition? We would always ask vendors

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:30.000
to give a sample for certain zip codes. We were mostly using demographic data, name and address at that time.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:36.000
And so we would say, okay, yeah, but okay, we're interested in buying this or leasing this data

00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:47.000
But we need a sample to see what's the quality of it. So we had a list of certain zip codes, and we would say, okay, here's a list of give us all the data you have for these zip codes

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:52.000
And that would allow us to look and see, oh, what was the quantity compared to other vendors

00:55:52.000 --> 00:56:01.000
We could look at the data, see what the quality is, and compare vendors and many vendors in the space selling this or leasing this data

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:06.000
So that gave us a way to plan and prepare and make a decision. And then

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:18.000
We also give us ready, you know to be prepared to receive it so that if we had to design certain applications or processes to use it

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:24.000
And again, as I mentioned, what are the limitations or restrictions on the use of it

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:26.000
And then you actually obtain it

00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:37.000
And you're either going to create or compiling is actually generating the data yourself. So that was another thing we often did, even though we leased a lot of data

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:44.000
We would compile some of our own data, and this was back in the day when people published telephone directories

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:50.000
So we would take and buy… we bought about 2,000 telephone directories from all over the United States

00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:57.000
Took them to a book bindery and they cut off the binding to make it all loose leaf

00:56:57.000 --> 00:57:04.000
And we bundle all the loose leaf pages of the telephone directory and shipped it to China

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:10.000
And in China, there would be these young women from the countryside that would come into town

00:57:10.000 --> 00:57:18.000
And there would be these companies that were set up with laptop computers, back in the 0286 days

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:24.000
And they would give one operator a page of the directory

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:32.000
And she would go in, and she didn't have to understand English. Here's an A on the page, and here's an A on the keyboard

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:35.000
of typing in all of that information

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:42.000
Then they would take the same page, go over to the second operator, let the second operator key in that page

00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:56.000
And then they would take the two keying operations and compare them, and that way they could… we could get almost 100% accuracy in the keying, because any discrepancy would go to a third person who would reconcile and get the correct values

00:57:56.000 --> 00:57:59.000
So

00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:04.000
That's called compiling. You're generating information yourself.

00:58:04.000 --> 00:58:10.000
We did that some, but we also bought or leased or rented a lot of data

00:58:10.000 --> 00:58:16.000
And you have to load those files. This was another big issue that was very cost intensive. When I left

00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:18.000
The company

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:23.000
There were 825 employees

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:25.000
that their only job

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:30.000
was to take, in that case was mostly a tape reel, a reel of tape

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:34.000
In some cases, it's a digital file of data

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:38.000
and inspect it and to look and see

00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:44.000
How would… what were the fields in the data, and were they aligned properly

00:58:44.000 --> 00:58:57.000
Because in our operation, however the data came in, it had to be mapped to a standard format. And so none of the data we were getting was already in that format

00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:14.000
So somebody had to look at it, inspect it, make a decision of how they were going to transform the data from the way it was received into the standard format that we wanted to use. And we were getting much… lots and lots of data, so it took more than 800 people to do that job every day.

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:25.000
of getting those files, looking at them, how is it aligned, and how are they going to be transformed into the standard format

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:36.000
And so basically a lot of our compiling was, well, semi-structured. So telephone directories are semi-structured. They're not fully structured

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:46.000
But then you have a lot of unstructured data, documents, for example, one of the things we were interested in

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:52.000
were corporate actions, and so the federal government requires corporations

00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:58.000
to file documentation when there's some major change in their business

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:01.000
For example, change of ownership

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:09.000
things like that. Then they filed a certain form, but that form was a narrative form. It wasn't a fielded form

01:00:09.000 --> 01:00:19.000
You just wrote in what had happened, and you could describe it. So, getting the information of who bought whom, which company bought which other company, and when

01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:25.000
Out of that required reading that document somehow and structuring that information

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:37.000
So that is a big issue that we're having now, and that's one of the major applications we're using for AI now, because now large language models can read and understand

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:48.000
So rather than having a person try to read a document and pull out information, we can now use large language models to read that data and identify information and pull it out

01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:54.000
So that's one of the major applications that we have right now of large language models.

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:59.000
And it can be applied also to images and to audio as well.

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:02.000
Even right now

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:04.000
I don't know in this

01:01:04.000 --> 01:01:10.000
Recording, I don't know, when you read the recording, you can see a subtitle, right? It's changing

01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:15.000
My voice and my narrative of the

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:19.000
that I'm talking, whatever I'm talking about, into

01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:22.000
Text, right? So audio to text

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:30.000
And you go same thing image detect with OCR

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:33.000
Okay, well

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:44.000
Yeah, we're still good on that. So then again, you know you have to store it and share it, and that's an operation all of itself of taking and designing

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:57.000
If you're using relational database now, we don't use that as much as we did. We don't worry as much about normalizing the data for big data operations. But still, we have to store it in some way

01:01:57.000 --> 01:02:00.000
And make a way to share it

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:10.000
And that's a double-edged sword, right? We have to make it available if people are not using it, we're not getting any value. But we have to be careful with whom we share it, right? Because

01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:14.000
We don't want to make it so available that anybody can come in and pick up our data

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:17.000
And have data leakage.

01:02:17.000 --> 01:02:29.000
And then we've already talked about maintaining it so that ensure that it stays up to date and everything continues to work properly. Many operations that we often do

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:34.000
To change parsing is a big thing where we have fields that are

01:02:34.000 --> 01:02:44.000
like a person's name, and we want to parse it out so that we separate out the first name, the middle name, last name, generational suffix, things like that

01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:46.000
standardize it so that

01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:52.000
street always conforms to a certain abbreviation ST that the Postal Service likes

01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:56.000
The point is, a lot of people put STR or spell out street

01:02:56.000 --> 01:03:04.000
So there's a lot of things that need to be done to maintain it, to put it in and then to keep it up to date.

01:03:04.000 --> 01:03:18.000
And then we may want to enhance it. Oftentimes we would take data and we wanted to know the gender, right? So there's lots of tools that we'll take and look at names and try to decide what the gender

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:21.000
associated with that name

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:34.000
And then a big one for us is deduplication. That's my main research area is how do I, when I'm getting duplicates of the same information, it may not be an exact duplicate

01:03:34.000 --> 01:03:41.000
It may be John, I mean, it may be Bill Smith, and another one may be William Smith, spelled differently, a nickname.

01:03:41.000 --> 01:03:46.000
But can I identify that this is really the same Bill or William Smith

01:03:46.000 --> 01:03:53.000
The same customer, the same patient, and deduplicate and gather those together that match

01:03:53.000 --> 01:04:02.000
And then merge together. And so again, looking at the hospital situation, I have all of these patient encounters

01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:09.000
I need to identify which of those encounters are for the same patient, merge those together to build a medical chart.

01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:21.000
So that I know from all the different visits that the patient has made what have been all the diagnostic tests, what have been all the recommendations, the prescriptions, and so forth that were made

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:23.000
into one, bring it all together

01:04:23.000 --> 01:04:35.000
And then apply, of course, is the main reason we're doing all of it. We're trying to build something, some product or service that's going to create value either internally or externally

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:44.000
The main product that we were building at Axiom, well, two main products, but one of the main products is that we were building an enhancement database

01:04:44.000 --> 01:04:47.000
By putting together all the information about consumers

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:57.000
things like, you know, were they married? Did they own a house, or were they renting? What kind of car do they own? All these many things, what were their hobbies, what were their interests

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:03.000
And not all of it didn't come from one place, so we got it from

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:05.000
probably about 80 different sources

01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:16.000
And then when it would come in, then the first thing we'd have to do is decide, are we talking about the same person? And if we are, then how do we merge together? Because sometimes there'd be disagreement

01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:31.000
Some sources would say, yes, this person owns a house. Another source would say, no, they're renting a house. And so we'd have to make decisions about which we felt was the most correct. Was it true? They were just at different times or was one incorrect

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:44.000
So, making all those decisions and merging those together to create, and so then what we had is for any particular customer, we could tell you about 1,000 different things about that customer

01:05:44.000 --> 01:05:54.000
And so people would come in and buy that, because banks especially would want to know, okay, we have this customer in our bank, but all we know is their name and their address

01:05:54.000 --> 01:06:05.000
You tell us whether they own the house or what kind of car, so we'll know how to build a model whether to offer them this kind of credit card or this kind of credit card, or not to offer them a credit card

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:08.000
So

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:15.000
That was one of the large products, information products that we built was this enhancement database

01:06:15.000 --> 01:06:18.000
But there are other things we did as well.

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:23.000
Other types of products and services. So

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:40.000
Lots of things. And then of course dispose. Typically, as I say, we often just archive it, we put it aside because we may need it later. But in some cases, regulations require us for us to destroy the information

01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:47.000
So supply cycle value, cost, and quality, as I mentioned earlier.

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:53.000
All of those phases require some cost to do, but only the apply phase

01:06:53.000 --> 01:06:59.000
Creates value or creates benefits and so potentially creates value for us

01:06:59.000 --> 01:07:02.000
So

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:07.000
And the information quality can be affected by activities in all of these phases

01:07:07.000 --> 01:07:16.000
So, information is a unique resource. As a raw material is not consumed. So, remember we said that a lot of what we have in information quality

01:07:16.000 --> 01:07:19.000
comes from manufacturing

01:07:19.000 --> 01:07:29.000
And so that's a good analogy. It's like, you know, we're we've got an information factory, we get raw products or raw materials, which are the raw data

01:07:29.000 --> 01:07:42.000
We put it through machinery, which is the programs and systems that we run, and then our end goal is to create some information product or service that we can sell or create value out of.

01:07:42.000 --> 01:07:49.000
And so, it's like a manufacturing operation when we do that. However, the analogy kind of falls down on two places

01:07:49.000 --> 01:07:54.000
that we're going to talk quite a bit about. One important place where it falls down is

01:07:54.000 --> 01:08:00.000
that in a real manufacturing process

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:05.000
When we start to build something, we have tolerances

01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:18.000
For the part, right? We're gonna build a car, we have certain diameter requirements for the axle, certain tensile strength, certain… in other words, and those understanding of those tolerances

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:28.000
is pretty intuitive. We understand the weight of something, we understand its tensile strength, we understand how big it is, how much it weighs

01:08:28.000 --> 01:08:41.000
You know, how long it is, what its diameter, all of those are physical things that we can understand. But when it comes to data, the characteristics of data, those dimensions of data, data quality

01:08:41.000 --> 01:08:43.000
are not as obvious, right?

01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:51.000
timeliness is not as obvious as the diameter of some a piece of equipment

01:08:51.000 --> 01:08:56.000
The other place, and this is what made me think of it, this slide

01:08:56.000 --> 01:09:03.000
Is that when we're building something like a car or toaster or whatever we're building, a manufactured product

01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:13.000
The raw materials that we bring in are consumed, right? We build cars, we're using up aluminum, we're using up steel, we build more cars, we have to buy more

01:09:13.000 --> 01:09:29.000
That's the good and the bad thing about data is that once we get data, we can use it over and over. We can copy, we can use as many copies as we want endlessly, right? Infinite amount. We're not using up the data when we process it

01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:32.000
In a program, it doesn't get used up

01:09:32.000 --> 01:09:43.000
That's a good thing in that it increases our marginal cost, right? If we can get data one time and sell copies of it a thousand times, then that's a great thing

01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:48.000
We can get huge marginal increases on the… the more we sell, the more we get.

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:52.000
However, the downside of that is if it's bad

01:09:52.000 --> 01:10:02.000
And we replicate bad data and get it out there, then some people say that like data is the new oil.

01:10:02.000 --> 01:10:08.000
And one argument is that, no, it's not like, well, because when you spill oil, you can clean it up

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:17.000
But when you spill data and your data gets out there, you can never get it back. So if it's bad data or if it's sensitive data and it goes out

01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:18.000
It's gone

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:22.000
And you can't get it back

01:10:22.000 --> 01:10:26.000
So raw materials not consumed

01:10:26.000 --> 01:10:33.000
You can easily copy it and use it over and over again. So quality is critical. Copies of bad data are bad data

01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:39.000
However, the advantage is the marginal value increases the more we use it

01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:45.000
And it's not a fungible asset. I don't know if you're familiar with the idea of fungibility in finance

01:10:45.000 --> 01:10:53.000
The fungible assets are those that are interchangeable. So if I have certain

01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:57.000
If I have barrels of oil, a certain type of crude oil

01:10:57.000 --> 01:11:05.000
There are different types of crude oil, but if they're the same type of crude oil, one barrel of oil is just like another barrel of oil.

01:11:05.000 --> 01:11:11.000
the barrels don't have identity to them, but that's generally not true with data

01:11:11.000 --> 01:11:14.000
Data tends to be non-fungible

01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:21.000
In the sense that we're describing entities like customers, like patients, like products

01:11:21.000 --> 01:11:29.000
And those are all unique and separately identifiable

01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:32.000
So they're not interchangeable

01:11:32.000 --> 01:11:38.000
So I think it's probably a good place to end today's lecture. So I'll be giving you

01:11:38.000 --> 01:11:46.000
That data will have an assignment coming up where we're going to start working with some data and looking at the quality of it

01:11:46.000 --> 01:11:54.000
Any quick questions or comments before we leave today?

01:11:54.000 --> 01:11:56.000
Okay

01:11:56.000 --> 01:11:58.000
If not, I'm going to

01:11:58.000 --> 01:11:59.000
Stop the record

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:01.000
I do.

